mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/assets/1df00f421b20b101d1500b4e2f47054f0c79dcfd2d82585fb8fb3735adb71af5.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=5.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=9?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9709dbf4b4dab3618c7bc97b3fefbdecbf04e55c3c9723c120eb446d2a9caea" "basis" 1525 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-121021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position of the particle at $t=9$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,9].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(9)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=5,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=9.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(9)=5+2=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=9$ is $x=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9709dbf4b4dab3618c7bc97b3fefbdecbf04e55c3c9723c120eb446d2a9caea" "basis" 1525 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-121021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differe